    "perplexity": {
      "description": "Theyesandeye is Lou Rhodes' fourth solo album, released in 2016 through Nude Records. The record continues her established approach as an acoustic folk singer-songwriter, building on the introspective, nature-focused aesthetic she developed across her previous releases. Recorded with co-producer Simon Byrt and featuring contributions from Ian Kellet (guitars), Nikolaj Bjerre (drums), Danny Keane (strings), and Tom Moth (harp), the album showcases subtly layered instrumentation that expands beyond the sparse arrangements of her earlier work. The 11 tracks were partially financed through Pledge Music and include a cover of \"Angels\" by The xx.\n\nThematically, Rhodes explores wonder and contemplation throughout the album, often invoking supernatural imagery to describe natural phenomena and human experience. The standout track \"Sea Organs\" exemplifies this approach, transforming observations of the natural world into an environmentalist meditation on environmental degradation. While Rhodes' vocal delivery remains warm and her guitar-driven compositions remain pleasant, the album maintains a consistent aesthetic rather than pushing her artistry in new directions. The instrumentation, though more intricate than previous efforts, employs strings, harps, and percussion in ways that feel deliberate but don't substantially deepen the emotional impact.\n\nThe album achieved modest commercial recognition, reaching number 100 on the UK charts. Though well-crafted and thematically coherent, Theyesandeye represents a refinement of Rhodes' established folk sensibility rather than a significant artistic departure, appealing primarily to listeners already familiar with her contemplative, acoustic-driven approach.",
